/*
    UIBuffContainer.uss
    Layout for the buff / debuff icon strips. Shared by UIBuff.uxml and UIDebuff.uxml.
    Colours that are not buff/debuff specific come from FishMMO-Theme.uss.
*/

/*
    BOTTOM CENTRE, GROWING OUTWARD FROM THE SCREEN'S MIDLINE. Buffs run left from the centre,
    debuffs run right from it, and the two share one 16-unit band directly above the resource
    row — so what a fight is currently doing to the player is read in a single glance at the spot
    the camera already looks at, rather than in two top corners the eye has to travel to.

    Each container is FIT-CONTENT and pinned by its INNER edge: buffs are `right: 50%`, debuffs
    are `left: 50%`. With `width: auto` the element is exactly as wide as the icons it holds, so
    its inner edge stays on the midline and it grows outward as entries arrive — and the drag
    handle stays the size of the strip. Every panel here is draggable (see the CanDrag override
    in UITKBuffContainer), and a full-width transparent band across the bottom of the screen
    would swallow every click that landed in it.

    Not `left: 50%; translate: -100% 0`, which pins the inner edge just as well but moves the
    painted box by a percentage of the element's OWN width — and that width changes every time an
    icon arrives, so a strip the player had dragged would crawl sideways on its own.
    SetDragPosition deliberately leaves `translate` in place (it measures and compensates rather
    than clears it), which is right for the fixed-size windows that rule was written for and
    wrong here.

    BOUNDED AND WRAPPING. max-width is what gives `flex-wrap` on .buff-list a limit to fold
    against; without one, a fit-content flex container grows along a single row for as long as it
    has icons. Measured at 1200x800 with the 40px icons of the time: twelve icons made a 528px
    strip and thirty made a 1320px one that ran off both edges of a 1200-wide screen, crossing
    the bars and the cast bar on the way.

    16px ICONS, DOWN FROM 28 — and that number is set by the worst case, not by taste. At 2px of
    gutter the pitch is 18 and half the panel is 600 wide, so 33 icons fit in the 594 available
    and the thirty the probe drives are ONE ROW. The old corner strips wrapped at eight per row,
    which made thirty entries four rows and 124 units of height; this is 16. They are still large
    in practice: 16 reference units is about 26 physical pixels at 1920x1080.

    What is ABOVE and BELOW the band is load-bearing, and every panel around it is a separate
    UIDocument that cannot be laid out relative to anything else: .res-bar sits 8px below at
    bottom:84, .castbar-root is 10px above at bottom:132, and .chat-panel's bottom edge is at 132
    so the strip passes under the log. Move an offset, an icon size or a margin here and those
    have to move with it. Every number is measured, not estimated — see UIResourceBar.uss.

    These offsets were `top: 12px` in the two top corners until the strips moved down here, which
    is why UIMinimap.uss carried a 136px top padding and .party-panel started at y144. Both gave
    way to a strip that is no longer up there: the map took its whole corner back, and the party
    frame the 15 units the target frame leaves it.

    Both strips are draggable; the icons are the handle. Dragging one writes an absolute left/top
    and releases the inner edge, so a placed strip stays placed and grows from where it was put.
*/
.buff-container {
    position: absolute;
    max-width: 600px;
    bottom: 106px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    background-color: var(--clear);
}

/* Inner edges on the midline: the half-row each side grows away from. The 6px of padding is the
   gutter they leave around the screen's centre, and it is inside the 600 rather than beside it,
   because 600 is what `right: 50%` puts between an edge of the panel and the midline. */
.buff-container--buff {
    right: 50%;
    padding-right: 6px;
}

.buff-container--debuff {
    left: 50%;
    padding-left: 6px;
}

/* Horizontal strip that holds the generated buff groups. The container's max-width is what
   gives this wrap a limit to fold against; without a limit the row never breaks. */
.buff-list {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

/* Both sides pack toward the midline, which is the whole point of the arrangement: the first
   icon of the newest row appears at the centre and the strip is read outward from there. The
   buffs' list packs right because the list sits in the left half; the debuffs' packs left
   because theirs sits in the right half. Only visible once a side has wrapped, which takes more
   than 33 icons. */
.buff-container--buff .buff-list {
    justify-content: flex-end;
}

.buff-container--debuff .buff-list {
    justify-content: flex-start;
}

/* A single buff/debuff entry. */
.buff-group {
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 3px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--abyss-500);
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-color: var(--abyss-800);
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The player's own strips are the small ones. Scoped to the two containers in this file rather
   than changed on .buff-group itself, because two other panels reuse that base at their own
   sizes: the target frame's buff/debuff lists take it at 28 (UITarget.uss pads the lists and
   nothing else), and .party-effects__strip restates it down to 20 of its own accord.

   margin-bottom goes to 0 with the smaller icon. It exists to space wrapped rows apart, and on a
   single row it only makes the strip's box 3 units taller than the icons in it — which is 3
   units of the 8 that separate it from the resource bar. Past 33 icons a second line appears and
   sits flush under the first. */
.buff-container > .buff-list > .buff-group {
    width: 16px;
    height: 16px;
    margin-right: 2px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

/* The stacks count, scaled with the icon it annotates: 8px was chosen against a 28px icon and
   would cover half of a 16px one. */
.buff-container > .buff-list > .buff-group > .buff-group__label {
    font-size: 7px;
}

/* Depleting duration fill (drawn behind the icon, height driven from C#). */
.buff-group__fill {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    height: 100%;
    background-color: var(--brand-press);
}

/* Buff icon sprite. */
.buff-group__icon {
    position: absolute;
    left: 2px;
    top: 2px;
    right: 2px;
    bottom: 2px;
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-to-fit;
}

/* Small name/stack label drawn at the bottom of the icon. */
.buff-group__label {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    font-size: 8px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: lower-center;
    color: var(--abyss-100);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 1px var(--edge-dark);
}

/* Positive buffs glow ember-orange; debuffs glow danger-red. */
.buff-container--buff .buff-group__fill {
    background-color: var(--brand-press);
}

.buff-container--debuff .buff-group {
    border-color: var(--danger-border);
}

.buff-container--debuff .buff-group__fill {
    background-color: var(--danger-fill);
}

/* Standalone debuff modifier (used where groups are mixed in one list, e.g. target frame). */
.buff-group--debuff {
    border-color: var(--danger-border);
}

.buff-group--debuff .buff-group__fill {
    background-color: var(--danger-fill);
}
